<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ignalina Nuclear Power Plant</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1983">1983</a>–<a href="/years/2009">2009</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ignalina Nuclear Power Plant was a Soviet-built nuclear power station at Visaginas in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, with two RBMK reactors of the type used at Chernobyl. Its first reactor began operating in 1983, and at its height it produced most of Lithuania's electricity. As a condition of joining the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>, Lithuania shut down the reactors in 2004 and 2009, and the plant is being decommissioned.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ignalina-nuclear-power-plant.jpg" alt="Ignalina Nuclear Power Plant" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Doktorfell · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nuclear_Reactor_Ignalina_Lithuania_(172881343).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> intended the Ignalina plant as a symbol of its technological progress. Preparations began in 1974 and fieldwork three years later. The plant had two RBMK-1500 reactors, water-cooled and graphite-moderated, each with two turbines built by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv">Kharkiv</a> turbine plant. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernobyl-disaster">Chernobyl disaster</a> of April 1986 the reactors were de-rated to 1,360 MW. Unit 1 came online in December 1983 and Unit 2 in August 1987, a year later than planned because of Chernobyl.</p><p>The town of Visaginas was built for the workers, about 5,000 of whom staffed the plant. It produced 70 percent of the electricity of the Lithuanian S.S.R. and exported power elsewhere in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. Cooling water came from Lake Drūkšiai, the largest lake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>When Unit 1 came online in December 1983 a design flaw of the RBMK was noticed for the first time: graphite-displacer tips on the control rods caused a power excursion when they were inserted. The problem was not addressed until a similar surge partly caused the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernobyl-disaster">Chernobyl disaster</a>, and the modifications that followed were tested at Ignalina in 1987 and 1988.</p><p>In 1999 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> agreed to close the units as a condition of joining the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>, citing the lack of a containment building as a high risk. The European Union agreed to pay 820 million euros towards decommissioning and compensation. Closure met fierce opposition because the plant gave income to most local residents. A 2008 referendum on keeping Unit 2 open until a replacement was built won 1,155,192 votes but failed to reach the required 50 percent turnout, and President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdas-adamkus">Valdas Adamkus</a> opposed it on the grounds of Lithuania's international commitments. Unit 1 closed on 31 December 2004 and Unit 2 on 31 December 2009.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The plant had accounted for 25 percent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>'s generating capacity and supplied about 70 percent of its electrical demand. Decommissioning was funded about 95 percent by the international community, through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> and a fund set up in 2001 under the European Bank for Reconstruction and Development, and 5 percent by Lithuania. The first phase ran from 2004 to 2013, and a second was scheduled for 2014 to 2029, so that the site could be reused by 2030.</p><p>A new plant at the site was discussed. Parliament passed a law in 2007 and the power companies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> agreed in 2008 to form a company for it, but the project stopped after a 2012 referendum gave no mandate to continue. Ignalina also served as a filming location for the 2019 television series Chernobyl.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ignalina Nuclear Power Plant” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
